<!-- ===== HERO ===== -->
<header class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis (2009–2019)</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of how book pricing strategies relate to customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction genres over a decade of Amazon bestseller data.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Dataset: 550 records (50/year × 11 years)</span>
      <span>Report Date: May 2026</span>
      <span>Audience: Management</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ===== SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW ===== -->
<section class="section" id="market-overview">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">High-level summary of the dataset: total records, genre distribution, average price, average rating, and engagement volume across the full decade.</p>

    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <div class="kpi-label">Total Bestsellers</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">550</div>
        <div class="kpi-context">351 unique titles across 11 years</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <div class="kpi-label">Average User Rating</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">4.62<span style="font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--text-secondary)">/5.0</span></div>
        <div class="kpi-context">Range: 3.3 – 4.9</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <div class="kpi-label">Average Price</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">$13.10</div>
        <div class="kpi-context">Range: $0 (free) – $105</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent">
        <div class="kpi-label">Average Reviews</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">11,953</div>
        <div class="kpi-context">Per bestseller entry</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card accent2">
        <div class="kpi-label">Genre Split</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">240 / 310</div>
        <div class="kpi-context">Fiction (43.6%) / Non-Fiction (56.4%)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h3>Genre Distribution Over Time</h3>
        <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="genreStackedChart"></canvas></div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h3>Price Segment Distribution</h3>
        <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="priceSegmentChart"></canvas></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-callout">
      <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Non-Fiction titles dominate the bestseller list (56.4% of all entries), but Fiction commands higher average review counts (15,684 vs. 9,065), suggesting Fiction readers engage more deeply. Average prices cluster in the $6–15 range, which captures 58.4% of all bestseller appearances.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 2: PRICE-RATING CORRELATION ===== -->
<section class="section" id="price-rating-correlation">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Quantify the statistical relationship between book price and customer satisfaction, including Pearson correlation coefficient and scatter visualization.</p>

    <div class="corr-stat">
      <span class="corr-value" style="color:var(--red)">r = -0.133</span>
      <span class="corr-label">Pearson Correlation<br>(Price × User Rating)</span>
    </div>

    <div class="corr-explanation">
      <strong>The correlation is weakly negative:</strong> across all 550 bestseller entries, higher-priced books tend to receive <em>slightly lower</em> ratings. The effect is small (r<sup>2</sup> ≈ 0.018, explaining only 1.8% of rating variance) but directionally consistent. This pattern holds for both Fiction (r = -0.103) and Non-Fiction (r = -0.135) independently, suggesting it is not a genre artifact. The relationship appears driven primarily by the cluster of low-to-moderate price points ($0–15) that achieve the highest ratings, while premium-priced books ($30+) show no commensurate rating uplift.
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Price vs. User Rating — Full Dataset Scatter (n = 550)</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="scatterChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Average Rating by Price Segment</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="priceSegmentRatingChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-callout">
      <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Free and low-priced books ($0–5) achieve the highest average ratings (4.71–4.77). Premium pricing ($30+) does not correlate with higher satisfaction. Pricing strategy should focus on competitive positioning within the $6–15 sweet spot rather than chasing premium price points.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 3: GENRE COMPARISON ===== -->
<section class="section" id="genre-comparison">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Compare average price, user rating, and review counts between Fiction and Non-Fiction genres. Highlight key differences in value perception.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h3>Genre Comparison — Key Metrics</h3>
        <table class="comp-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Metric</th>
              <th>Fiction</th>
              <th>Non-Fiction</th>
              <th>Delta</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Total Entries</td>
              <td class="highlight">240</td>
              <td class="highlight">310</td>
              <td>—</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Average Price</td>
              <td class="highlight better">$10.85</td>
              <td>$14.84</td>
              <td class="worse">+$3.99 (36.8%)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Average Rating</td>
              <td class="highlight better">4.648</td>
              <td>4.595</td>
              <td class="better">+0.053</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Average Reviews</td>
              <td class="highlight better">15,684</td>
              <td>9,065</td>
              <td class="better">+6,619 (73.0%)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Price-Rating Correlation</td>
              <td>r = -0.103</td>
              <td>r = -0.135</td>
              <td>—</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Price Range</td>
              <td>$0 – $82</td>
              <td>$0 – $105</td>
              <td>—</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Most Common Price</td>
              <td>$8</td>
              <td>$11</td>
              <td>—</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h3>Price Distribution by Genre</h3>
        <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="genrePriceDistChart"></canvas></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Side-by-Side: Rating &amp; Price by Genre (Annual)</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="genreAnnualChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-callout">
      <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> Fiction delivers better perceived value — lower average prices ($10.85 vs. $14.84), higher average ratings (4.648 vs. 4.595), and dramatically higher engagement (73% more reviews). Non-Fiction commands a pricing premium but does not convert that premium into proportionally higher satisfaction or engagement.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION 4: TEMPORAL TRENDS (CRITICAL) ===== -->
<section class="section" id="temporal-trends">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
    <p class="section-objective">Analyze how average book prices, user ratings, and review volumes have evolved annually from 2009 to 2019. Identify inflection points and notable shifts in market dynamics.</p>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Combined Trend View: Price, Rating &amp; Review Volume (2009–2019)</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="combinedTrendChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Year-over-Year Price Change ($)</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="yoyPriceChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Genre-Specific Price Trends</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="genrePriceTrendChart"></canvas></div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="chart-card">
